{"surah": 62, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "يُسَبِّحُ لِلَّهِ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ ٱلْمَلِكِ ٱلْقُدُّوسِ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْحَكِيمِ ", "translation": "Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth is exalting Allāh,[1] the Sovereign, the Pure, the Exalted in Might, the Wise.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُسَبِّحُ", "translit": "ُḥِّbَsُy", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلِكِ", "translit": "ِkِlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْقُدُّوسِ", "translit": "ِswُّdُqْlٱ", "root": "قدس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزِ", "translit": "ِzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "هُوَ ٱلَّذِى بَعَثَ فِى ٱلْأُمِّيِّۦنَ رَسُولًا مِّنْهُمْ يَتْلُوا۟ عَلَيْهِمْ ءَايَٰتِهِۦ وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَٱلْحِكْمَةَ وَإِن كَانُوا۟ مِن قَبْلُ لَفِى ضَلَٰلٍ مُّبِينٍ ", "translation": "It is He who has sent among the unlettered [Arabs] a Messenger from themselves reciting to them His verses and purifying them and teaching them the Book [i.e., the Qur’ān] and wisdom [i.e., the sunnah] - although they were before in clear error -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَعَثَ", "translit": "َhtَʿعَb", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأُمِّيِّۦنَ", "translit": "َnۦِّyِّmُaʾْlٱ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَتْلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlْtَy", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِّkَzُyَw", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhُmِّlَʿعُyَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱلْحِكْمَةَ", "translit": "َẗَmْkِḥْlٱَw", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مُّبِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَءَاخَرِينَ مِنْهُمْ لَمَّا يَلْحَقُوا۟ بِهِمْۚ وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ ", "translation": "And [to] others of them who have not yet joined them. And He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَءَاخَرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrَhkāَءَw", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَلْحَقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَḥْlَy", "root": "لحق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزُ", "translit": "ُzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ فَضْلُ ٱللَّهِ يُؤْتِيهِ مَن يَشَآءُۚ وَٱللَّهُ ذُو ٱلْفَضْلِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ ", "translation": "That is the bounty of Allāh, which He gives to whom He wills, and Allāh is the possessor of great bounty.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَضْلُ", "translit": "ُlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُؤْتِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ذُو", "translit": "wُhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْفَضْلِ", "translit": "ِlْḍَfْlٱ", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَظِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِẓَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "مَثَلُ ٱلَّذِينَ حُمِّلُوا۟ ٱلتَّوْرَىٰةَ ثُمَّ لَمْ يَحْمِلُوهَا كَمَثَلِ ٱلْحِمَارِ يَحْمِلُ أَسْفَارًۢاۚ بِئْسَ مَثَلُ ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلَّذِينَ كَذَّبُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِۚ وَٱللَّهُ لَا يَهْدِى ٱلْقَوْمَ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "The example of those who were entrusted with the Torah and then did not take it on[1] is like that of a donkey who carries volumes [of books].[2] Wretched is the example of the people who deny the signs of Allāh. And Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حُمِّلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِّmُḥ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلتَّوْرَىٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰàَrْwَّtlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَحْمِلُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُlِmْḥَy", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَمَثَلِ", "translit": "ِlَhtَmَk", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْحِمَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَmِḥْlٱ", "root": "حمر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَحْمِلُ", "translit": "ُlِmْḥَy", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَسْفَارًۢا", "translit": "āًۢrāَfْsَaʾ", "root": "سفر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِb", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَذَّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يَهْدِى", "translit": "àِdْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "قُلْ يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ هَادُوٓا۟ إِن زَعَمْتُمْ أَنَّكُمْ أَوْلِيَآءُ لِلَّهِ مِن دُونِ ٱلنَّاسِ فَتَمَنَّوُا۟ ٱلْمَوْتَ إِن كُنتُمْ صَٰدِقِينَ ", "translation": "Say, \"O you who are Jews, if you claim that you are allies of Allāh, excluding the [other] people, then wish for death, if you should be truthful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَادُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُdāَh", "root": "هود"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "زَعَمْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْmَʿعَz", "root": "زعم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَوْلِيَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَyِlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَتَمَنَّوُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwَّnَmَtَf", "root": "مني"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتَ", "translit": "َtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "صَٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَٰṣ", "root": "صدق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَلَا يَتَمَنَّوْنَهُۥٓ أَبَدًۢا بِمَا قَدَّمَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْۚ وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌۢ بِٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "But they will not wish for it, ever, because of what their hands have put forth. And Allāh is Knowing of the wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَتَمَنَّوْنَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَnْwَّnَmَtَy", "root": "مني"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَبَدًۢا", "translit": "āًۢdَbَaʾ", "root": "ابد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَدَّمَتْ", "translit": "ْtَmَّdَq", "root": "قدم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَيْدِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِdْyَaʾ", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱِb", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "قُلْ إِنَّ ٱلْمَوْتَ ٱلَّذِى تَفِرُّونَ مِنْهُ فَإِنَّهُۥ مُلَٰقِيكُمْۖ ثُمَّ تُرَدُّونَ إِلَىٰ عَٰلِمِ ٱلْغَيْبِ وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ فَيُنَبِّئُكُم بِمَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"Indeed, the death from which you flee - indeed, it will meet you. Then you will be returned to the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, and He will inform you about what you used to do.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتَ", "translit": "َtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَفِرُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّrِfَt", "root": "فرر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُلَٰقِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِqَٰlُm", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُرَدُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّdَrُt", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَٰلِمِ", "translit": "ِmِlَٰʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱ", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَdَٰhَّhslٱَw", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَيُنَبِّئُكُم", "translit": "mُkُyʾِّbَnُyَf", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَt", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِذَا نُودِىَ لِلصَّلَوٰةِ مِن يَوْمِ ٱلْجُمُعَةِ فَٱسْعَوْا۟ إِلَىٰ ذِكْرِ ٱللَّهِ وَذَرُوا۟ ٱلْبَيْعَۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ خَيْرٌ لَّكُمْ إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, when [the adhān] is called for the prayer on the day of Jumuʿah [Friday], then proceed to the remembrance of Allāh and leave trade. That is better for you, if you only knew.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُودِىَ", "translit": "َàِdwُn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlِl", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْجُمُعَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَʿعُmُjgْlٱ", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَٱسْعَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَʿعْsٱَf", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ذِكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَذَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَhdَw", "root": "وذر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْبَيْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْyَbْlٱ", "root": "بيع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "فَإِذَا قُضِيَتِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةُ فَٱنتَشِرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱبْتَغُوا۟ مِن فَضْلِ ٱللَّهِ وَٱذْكُرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ كَثِيرًا لَّعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ ", "translation": "And when the prayer has been concluded, disperse within the land and seek from the bounty of Allāh, and remember Allāh often that you may succeed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُضِيَتِ", "translit": "ِtَyِḍُq", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةُ", "translit": "ُẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَٱنتَشِرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrِhsَtnٱَf", "root": "نشر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱبْتَغُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُغَtْbٱَw", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَضْلِ", "translit": "ِlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱذْكُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُkْhdٱَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَثِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَّعَلَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّlَʿعَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تُفْلِحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِlْfُt", "root": "فلح"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَإِذَا رَأَوْا۟ تِجَٰرَةً أَوْ لَهْوًا ٱنفَضُّوٓا۟ إِلَيْهَا وَتَرَكُوكَ قَآئِمًاۚ قُلْ مَا عِندَ ٱللَّهِ خَيْرٌ مِّنَ ٱللَّهْوِ وَمِنَ ٱلتِّجَٰرَةِۚ وَٱللَّهُ خَيْرُ ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ ", "translation": "But [on one occasion] when they saw a transaction or a diversion, [O Muḥammad], they rushed to it and left you standing. Say, \"What is with Allāh is better than diversion and than a transaction, and Allāh is the best of providers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَأَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَaʾَr", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تِجَٰرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrَٰjgِt", "root": "تجر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَهْوًا", "translit": "āًwْhَl", "root": "لهو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱنفَضُّوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُّḍَfnٱ", "root": "فضض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَتَرَكُوكَ", "translit": "َkwُkَrَtَw", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَآئِمًا", "translit": "āًmِyʾٓāَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهْوِ", "translit": "ِwْhَّllٱ", "root": "لهو"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلتِّجَٰرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrَٰjgِّtlٱ", "root": "تجر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِzَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رزق"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"4": [{"arabic": "وإن اشتركوا في مصيبة الحرمان من حقيقة الأمر فالحقيقة فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء والله ذو الفضل العظيم وحكمته يختص بها من يشاء ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتي خيرا كثيرا ولنعد إلى الغرض فقد أرخينا الطول وطولنا النفس في أمر هو أعلاى من علوم المعاملات التي نقصدها في هذا الكتاب فقد ظهر أن رتبة الهلاك ليس إلا للجهال المكذبين وشهادة ذلك من كتاب الله وسنة رسوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا تدخل تحت الحصر فلذلك لم نوردها الرتبة الثانية رتبة المعذبين وهذه رتبة من تحلى بأصل الإيمان ولكن قصر في الوفاء بمقتضاه فإن رأس الإيمان هو التوحيد وهو أن لا يعبد", "english": "The fire of Hell is the coldest of fires [in comparison to the fire of separation]. And you should not deny this in the world of the Hereafter, for it has a witnessed parallel in the world of this world. Indeed, one has been seen who was overcome by ecstasy (wajd) and walked upon fire and upon the roots of cane, his feet being cut, yet he did not feel it, due to the extreme dominance of what was in his heart. And you see the angry man, when anger overcomes him in battle, receiving wounds and not feeling them at that moment, because anger is a fire in the heart. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: {«الغضب قطعة من النار»} \"Anger is a piece of fire.\" The burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) is more severe than the burning of bodies, and the more severe annuls the sensation of the weaker, as you observe. For destruction from fire and the sword is only inasmuch as they separate between two parts that are linked one to the other by the bond of composition (ta'līf) possible in bodies. So that which separates between the heart and its Beloved, to which it is linked by a bond of composition far more firm than the composition of bodies, is more intensely painful—if you are among the people of insight (abṣār) and the people of hearts. And it is not far-fetched that he who has no heart should not perceive the severity of this pain and should deem it trivial in comparison to bodily pain. For a boy, if he were given the choice between the pain of being deprived of a ball and a polo-stick, and the pain of being deprived of the rank of a Sultan, he would not feel the pain of deprivation from the Sultan's rank at all, nor would he count that as a pain. And the enemy in the field with the polo-stick is more beloved to him than a thousand thrones of the Sultan with sitting upon them. Nay, even one overcome by the lust of the belly, if given the choice between porridge (harīsah) and sweets (ḥalwā) and between a beautiful deed by which he conquers enemies and delights friends, he would prefer the porridge and sweets. All of this is due to the absence of the meaning (maʿnā) by whose existence rank becomes beloved, and the presence of the meaning by whose existence food becomes delicious. This is for one whom the attributes of beasts and predators have enslaved, and in whom the attributes of the angels have not appeared—those attributes which are not suited by, nor delight in, anything but proximity to the Lord of the Worlds, and which are pained only by distance and the veil (ḥijāb). And just as taste is only in the tongue and hearing only in the ears, so this attribute is only in the heart. So he who has no heart has no such sense, like he who has no hearing or sight has no pleasure in melodies or the beauty of forms and colors. And not every human has a heart; were it so, the saying of God, Most High, would not be sound: {«إن في ذلك لذكرى لمن كان له قلب»} \"Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart\" (Q. 50:37). Thus He made the one who does not heed the Quran devoid of a heart. And by \"heart\" I do not mean this [organ] which the ribs of the chest encompass. Rather, I mean the secret (sirr) which is of the world of the Command (ʿālam al-amr). The flesh which is of the world of Creation (ʿālam al-khalq) is its Throne (ʿarsh), the chest is its Footstool (kursī), and the rest of the limbs are its domain and kingdom. And to God belongs the Creation and the Command altogether. But that secret, concerning which God, Most High, said: {«قل الروح من أمر ربي»} \"Say: The Spirit is of the Command of my Lord\" (Q. 17:85), is the commander and the king, because between the world of the Command and the world of Creation there is an ordering, and the world of the Command is commander over the world of Creation. It is the subtle divine reality (al-laṭīfah) which, when it is sound, the rest of the body is sound for its sake. Whoever knows it has known his own self, and whoever knows his own self has known his Lord. At that point, the servant smells the beginnings of the fragrance of the meaning folded under his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: {«إن الله خلق آدم على صورته»} \"God created Adam upon His image.\" He looks with the eye of mercy upon those who adhere to its literal wording and upon those who are obstinate in the path of its interpretation (ta'wīl). And even though His mercy for those adhering to the letter is greater than His mercy for the obstinate interpreters, because mercy is proportional to the affliction, and the affliction of those [literalists] is greater—even if they share in the affliction of being deprived of the reality of the matter—for the Reality (al-ḥaqīqah) is the bounty of God which He gives to whom He wills, and God is the Possessor of Great Bounty. And His Wisdom is singled out for whom He wills: {«ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتي خيرا كثيرا»} \"And whoever is given wisdom has been given much good\" (Q. 2:269). Let us return to the purpose, for we have drawn out the length and lengthened the breath in a matter that is higher than the sciences of transactions (muʿāmalāt) which we intend in this book. It has become clear that the rank of destruction is only for the ignorant deniers. And the testimony of that from the Book of God and the Sunnah of His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him, is beyond enumeration, so we have not cited it.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 930", "section_title_en": "The Ranks of the Hereafter: On the Distribution of Degrees and the States of the Saved, the Punished, and the Victorious", "section_index": 930, "anchors": "- Root: ح ر ق (ḥ-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, and the fire's consumption of its fuel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The fire of the Hereafter (Nār) is a created reality, but its perception is tied to the spiritual state of the perceiver.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical burning; metaphorical (Majaz) for the burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) by overwhelming love or anger.\n- Root: ق ل ب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart (Qalb) is the seat of perception and understanding, distinct from the physical organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the physical heart; metaphorical for the spiritual subtlety (Laṭīfah) that is the locus of divine knowledge and love.\n- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know, to perceive a thing by its true nature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge (ʿIlm) is the apprehension of a thing's reality, which is the basis for distinguishing the ranks of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for acquired knowledge; metaphorical for the \"unveiling\" (Kashf) knowledge granted to the knowers (ʿĀrifūn).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — Used to distinguish the specific referents of terms like \"heart\" (Qalb) and \"faith\" (Īmān), ensuring the precise meaning is not conflated with common usage.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم ذلك فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء (1) وقد استشهد ابن عطاء أيضا لما سئل عن ذلك فقال الغني أفضل لأنه وصف الحق أما دليله الأول ففيه نظر لأن الخبر قد ورد مفصلا تفصيلا يدل على خلاف ذلك وهو أن ثواب الفقير في التسبيح يزيد على ثواب الغني وأن فوزهم بذلك الثواب فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء فقد روى زيد بن أسلم عن أنس بن مالك رضي الله عنه قال بعث الفقراء رسولا إلا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال إني رسول الفقراء إليك فقال {مرحبا} بك وبمن جئت من عندهم قوم أحبهم قال قالوا يا رسول الله إن", "english": "Evidence for this may be found in what is related in the report (khabar) that the poor complained to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, that the rich had preceded them in good deeds, alms, pilgrimage (ḥajj), and holy struggle (jihād). So he taught them words of glorification (tasbīḥ) and told them that by these they would attain what surpassed what the rich had attained. Then the rich learned these words and began to say them, so the poor returned to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and informed him [of this]. He said, peace and blessings be upon him: {«ذلك فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء»} — \"That is the bounty of God; He gives it to whom He wills.\" (1)", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1074", "section_title_en": "On the Relative Excellence of Poverty and Wealth — A Detailed Exposition", "section_index": 1074, "anchors": "- Root: فقر (fa-qā-rā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of neediness, destitution; the state of one lacking provision or means.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fourfold division — (1) essential need before God (universal to all creatures), (2) lack of acquired possessions, (3) contentment of the soul, (4) metaphorical usage. Here, the operative sense is the second: absence of worldly goods.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — absence of wealth; Metaphorical (Majaz) — \"فقرة الكلام\" (a rhetorical gem/point), showing the root's capacity for extension. The discussion here remains in the literal register of material need.\n- Root: غني (ghayn-nūn-yā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of sufficiency, independence, freedom from need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Threefold division — (1) absolute freedom from all needs (God alone), (2) fewness of needs (the praised \"wealth of the soul\"), (3) abundance of possessions. The debate here pivots between senses (2) and (3).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"أغنى عنه\" (to be independent of); Metaphorical — \"تغنته القيود\" (fetters \"enriched\" him, i.e., clung to him), showing that attachment, not mere possession, is the real issue.\n- Root: صبر (ṣād-bā-rā)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Restraint of the soul in the face of hardship; steadfastness in obedience and abstinence from sin. The \"patient poor\" (فقير صابر) is the one who holds fast without anxious striving.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — endurance of affliction; the compound \"صابر محتسب\" (patient and reckoning on God's reward) is the technical Quranic-Hadith register.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Prophet's saying {«ليس الغنى عن كثرة العرض إنما الغنى غنى النفس»} uses \"إنما\" to restrict true wealth to soul-contentment, negating the apparent sense of abundance. This syntactic restriction governs the entire argument: the \"wealth\" that matters is a state of the heart, not of the purse.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "5": [{"arabic": "{مثل الذين حملوا التوراة ثم لم يحملوها كمثل الحمار يحمل أسفارا} أراد به علماء اليهود وقال في بلعم بن باعوراء {واتل عليهم نبأ الذي آتيناه آياتنا فانسلخ منها} حتى بلغ {فمثله كمثل الكلب إن تحمل عليه يلهث أو تتركه يلهث} قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما أوتي بلعم كتابا فأخلد إلى شهوات الأرض أي سكن حبه إليها فمثله بالكلب {إن تحمل عليه يلهث أو تتركه يلهث} أي سواء آتيته الحكمة أو لم أوته لا يدع شهوته ويكفي العالم هذا الخطر فأي عالم لم يتبع شهوته وأي عالم لم يأمر بالخير الذي لا يأتيه فمهما خطر للعالم عظم قدره بالإضافة إلى الج", "english": "For this reason, Kaʿb al-Aḥbār said: \"Knowledge has a tyranny like the tyranny of wealth.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 868", "section_title_en": "The Sixth Cause of Pride: Pride in Knowledge — Its Danger and the First Remedy", "section_index": 868, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of bigness in stature, age, or rank; \"كبر عليه\" = to be grievous/heavy upon one.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Kabīr and al-ṣaghīr are relative terms (mutadāyifān) — a thing is small beside one thing and great beside another; used of both connected quantity (bodies) and disconnected quantity (number).\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"Kabar al-amr\" = the matter became great; \"kabura ʿalayhi\" = it was burdensome. Metaphorical (majāz): applied to rank, honor, and station, not merely physical size.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perception and apprehension of a thing as it truly is; the root also yields \"ʿalam\" (sign/marker) and \"maʿlam\" (landmark) — knowledge as that by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ʿilm: idrāk al-shayʾ bi-ḥaqīqatihi\" — apprehension of a thing in its true reality; two kinds: (1) apprehension of the essence of a thing, (2) judgment upon a thing affirming or denying something of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Huwa min aʿlām al-ʿilm al-khāfiqah\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; \"maʿālim al-ṭarīq\" = the traces by which the road is recognized. Knowledge is that which marks and distinguishes.\n- Root: خطر (kh-ṭ-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be in peril; \"khaṭar\" = danger, risk; also \"khaṭara bi-nafsihi\" = to hazard oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not separately glossed in the retrieved entry, but the Qur'anic usage (e.g., \"akhṭara\" in the sense of grave consequence) aligns with the notion of imminent peril.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Khaṭar\" = danger/risk; \"rajul khaṭīr\" = a man of great consequence — the same root yields both \"peril\" and \"high station,\" a deliberate lexical pairing Ghazali exploits here.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure here (\"فإنهما كمالان في النفس جديران بأن يفرح بهما\") employs embedded relative clauses and prepositional phrases that must be unpacked in English without losing the logical subordination.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "6": [{"arabic": "لأحببته وتلا قوله تعالى فتمنوا الموت إن كنتم صادقين فقال الرجل فقد قال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يتمنينأحدكم الموت (3) فقال إنما قاله لضر نزل به لأن الرضا بقضاء الله تعالى أفضل من طلب الفرار منه فإن قلت من لا يحب الموت فهل يتصور أن يكون محبا لله فأقول كراهة الموت قد تكون لحب الدنيا والتأسف على فراق الأهل والمال والولد وهذا ينافي كمال حب الله تعالى لأن الحب الكامل هو الذي يستغرق كل القلب ولكن لا يبعد أن يكون له مع حب الأهل والولد شائبة من حب الله تعالى ضعيفة فإن الناس متفاوتون في الحب ويدل على ا", "english": "He, peace be upon him, said: «من أحب لقاء الله أحب الله لقاءه» — \"Whoever loves the meeting with God, God loves meeting with him.\" And Ḥudhayfah said at the moment of death: \"A beloved has come upon the occasion of need; he who regrets has not succeeded.\"", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1175", "section_title_en": "The Signs and Proofs of Love for God — Section 1175", "section_index": 1175, "anchors": "- Root: ح ب ب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: to love, to cherish; al-ḥabīb is the beloved; maḥabbah denotes the inclination of the heart toward that which pleases it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Maḥabbah of God for the servant is His willing good for him and drawing him near; the servant's love for God is his inclination toward obedience and his yearning for vision (ru'yah) of Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): the heart's attachment; metaphorical (majāz): love as expressed through action and sacrifice, since mere claim without proof is empty.\n- Root: م و ت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of corporeal life; the transition from this abode.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is not annihilation but departure from the body — a relocation of the soul; hence the believer's love of death is love of meeting God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: biological death; metaphorical: the \"death\" of appetites and the severance of worldly attachments as a spiritual state.\n- Root: ق ت ل (q-t-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Killing, slaying; qitāl is combat.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Martyrdom (shahādah) in God's path is the supreme proof of sincere love, as it offers the self entirely.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical combat; metaphorical: the struggle against the lower self (jihād al-nafs) as the greater warfare.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish genuine love from mere claim, restricting true love to that which is evidenced by action.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "8": [{"arabic": "قال الله فيهم قل إن الموت الذي تفرون منه فإنه ملاقيكم ثم تردون إلى عالم الغيب والشهادة فينبئكم بما كنتم تعملون ثم الناس إما منهمك وإما تائب مبتدئ أو عارف منته أما المنهمك فلا يذكر الموت وإن ذكره فيذكره للتأسف على دنياه ويشتغل بمذمته وهذا يزيده ذكر الموت من الله بعدا وأما التائب فإنه يكثر من ذكر الموت لينبعث به من قلبه الخوف والخشية فيفي بتمام التوبة وربما يكره الموت خيفة من أن يختطفه قبل تمام التوبة وقبل إصلاح الزاد وهو معذور في كراهة الموت ولا يدخل هذا تحت قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم من كره لقاء الله كره الله لقاءه (2) فإن هذا ليس يكره الموت ولقاء ا", "english": "Know that the one engrossed in this world, bent over its delusions, loving its lusts — his heart is inevitably heedless of the remembrance of death, so he does not remember it. And when he is reminded of it, he detests it and recoils from it. These are they of whom God said: {«قُلْ إِنَّ الْمَوْتَ الَّذِي تَفِرُّونَ مِنْهُ فَإِنَّهُ مُلَاقِيكُمْ ثُمَّ تُرَدُّونَ إِلَىٰ عَالِمِ الْغَيْبِ وَالشَّهَادَةِ فَيُنَبِّئُكُم بِمَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ»} — \"Say: Indeed, the death from which you flee — it will surely meet you. Then you will be returned to the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, and He will inform you of what you used to do.\" Then people are either one engrossed [in the world], or a penitent beginner, or a gnostic who has reached the end.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1282", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Remembrance of Death — Section 1282: The First Part on Its Premises and Consequences up to the Blast of the Trumpet, Containing Eight Chapters", "section_index": 1282, "anchors": "- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of death as the cessation of life; Al-Khalil notes its usage as a natural disposition (ka-l-ṭabīʿah fīhā), with derivatives like *mayyit* (dead), *mawtān* (deathly state).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is the opposite of life; in theological terms, it is the separation of the soul from the body, and the Quranic usage extends to spiritual death — the heart's heedlessness of God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*): physical death and dying. Metaphorical (*majāz*): \"reviving the dead land\" (i.e., barren earth) and \"dead-hearted\" man — one lacking spiritual vitality.\n- Root: ذكر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, recollection, and mention; also used for praise and invocation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Dhikr* is the presence of a thing in the mind; in the Quranic context, it is the heart's vigilance against forgetfulness (*nisyān*), and its highest form is the constant awareness of God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: verbal mention. Metaphorical: inward remembrance that produces fear and longing — as in the *dhikr* of death stirring the heart.\n- Root: توب (t-w-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Return (*rujūʿ*) from sin; the penitent (*tāʾib*) is one who turns back to God.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Tawbah* is returning from error to obedience, requiring three elements: remorse, cessation, and resolve. Its completion (*tamām*) is the state of unwavering commitment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning back. Metaphorical: the soul's aversion from worldly allurements toward the divine presence.\n- Root: عرف (ʿ-r-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge, acquaintance, and recognition; *ʿārif* is one who knows by direct experience.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Maʿrifah* is the soul's apprehension of realities; the *ʿārif* (gnostic) is one whose knowledge has become a settled state of witnessing, not mere report.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowing something after ignorance. Metaphorical: the *ʿārif* who \"knows\" death as the tryst with the Beloved — knowledge transmuted into love.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle *innamā* restricts predication to a single subject, as in \"إنما يخاف فوت لقاء الله\" — confining the penitent's fear solely to missing the divine encounter, not to death itself.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وأما المنافقون ففروا من الزحف خوفا من الموت فقيل لهم إن الموت الذين تفرون منه فإنه ملاقيكم فإيثارهم البقاء على الشهادة استبدال الذي هو أدنى بالذي هو خير فأولئك الذين اشتروا الضلالة بالهدى فما ربحت تجارتهم وما كانوا مهتدين وأما المخلصون فإن الله تعالى اشترى منهم أنفسهم وأموالهم بأن لهم الجنة فلما رأوا أنهم تركوا تمتع عشرين سنة مثلا أو ثلاثين سنة بتمتع الأبد استبشروا ببيعهم الذي بايعوا به فهذا بيان المزهود فيه وإذا فهمت هذا علمت أن ما ذكره المتكلمون في حد الزهد لم يشيروا به إلا إلى بعض أقسامه فذكر كل واحد منهم ما رآه غالبا على نف", "english": "...or the attainment of the rank of martyrdom (al-shahādah). And whosoever among them died upon his bed would grieve bitterly over the loss of martyrdom — so much so that Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah Most High be well pleased with him, when death approached him upon his bed, kept saying: \"How often did I deceive my soul [into battle], and charge upon the ranks, coveting martyrdom — and now I die the death of old women!\" When he died, eight hundred wounds from the marks of injuries were counted upon his body. Such was the state of the truthful ones in faith — may Allah Most High be well pleased with them all.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1093", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Degrees of Asceticism (Zuhd) and Its Divisions — Relative to Itself, to That Which Is Renounced, and to That Which Is Sought", "section_index": 1093, "anchors": "- Root: زهد (Z-H-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: scarcity, paucity, and abstemiousness; al-zuhd is also \"estimation/assessment\" (al-hazr), as in assessing a palm-crop.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الزهيد: الشيء القليل\" — the ascetic (al-zāhid) is one who turns away from a thing, content with the little (al-qalīl) thereof; theological nuance: renunciation is a voluntary contentment with the lesser in exchange for the greater.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (literal): \"زهد في الشيء: رغب عنه\" — to turn away from a thing with aversion. Majaz (metaphorical): \"رجل زهيد: قليل الخير\" — a man of little good; \"اد زهيد\" — a valley that takes little water. Thus zuhd is literal renunciation, but its degrees vary by the object renounced.\n- Root: قصر (Q-S-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To restrain, confine, hold back; also shortness as opposed to length.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القصر: خلاف الطول\" — shortness is the opposite of length; both are relative terms (mutadāyifān) defined by comparison to another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"قصرته: حبسته\" — I restrained him. \"وقصرت نفسي على هذا الأمر\" — I confined myself to this matter, not aspiring beyond it. Majaz: \"أقصر المطر: أقلع\" — the rain ceased; \"قصر الأمل\" — the shortening of hope/aspiration, a metaphorical restraint of the soul's reach.\n- Root: نفس (N-F-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which one desires to be near.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul is the spirit; in divine attribution, \"نفسه\" denotes His very essence (dhāt), not a separate entity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"شيء نفيس\" — a precious thing. Majaz: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — \"he poured out his self\" meaning his blood; thus nafs can denote the entire vital being given in sacrifice.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — When multiple predicates are attributed to a single subject (zuhd), each speaker's definition is a restricted specification (takhṣīṣ) of one division, not a comprehensive definition (ḥadd) of the whole genus. The syntactic pattern \"إنما هو كذا\" effects exclusive predication, signaling partial scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}], "9": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قال الله تعالى {إذا نودي للصلاة من يوم الجمعة فاسعوا إلى ذكر الله وذروا البيع} فحرم الاشتغال بأمور الدنيا وبكل صارف عن السعي إلى الجمعة وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله عز وجل فرض عليكم الجمعة في يومي هذا في مقامي هذا (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم من ترك الجمعة ثلاثا من غير عذر طبع الله على قلبه (2) وفي لفظ آخر فقد نبذ الإسلام وراء ظهره (3) واختلف رجل إلى ابن عباس يسأله عن رجل مات لم يكن يشهد جمعة ولا جماعة فقال في النار فلم يزل يتردد إليه شهرا يسأله عن ذلك وهو يقول في النار وفي الخبر إن أهل الكتابين أعطوا يوم", "english": "Know that this is a momentous day, by which Allah magnified Islam and singled out the Muslims exclusively. Allah, the Exalted, said: {«O you who believe! When the call is proclaimed for the prayer on Friday, hasten earnestly to the remembrance of Allah and leave off business»} (al-Jumuʿah 62:9). Thus, He prohibited engagement in worldly affairs and every distraction that turns one away from hastening to the Friday [prayer].", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 145", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquettes of Recitation, Section 145 (Continuation) & Chapter V: On the Excellence, Etiquettes, and Conditions of Friday (Jumuʿah)", "section_index": 145, "anchors": "- Root: رفع (rafʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To raise, elevate, lift up; also to remove or abolish.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Elevation in rank or physical lifting; in ritual context, the raising of hands (rafʿ al-yadayn) as a physical gesture signifying supplication and humility before the Divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) physical lifting of the hands; metaphorical (majāz) elevation of one's station or the removal of a burden.\n- Root: سعى (saʿy)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To walk quickly, to strive, to exert oneself; to run toward a goal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Striving and hastening toward an objective; in the Quranic context of Friday, it denotes the earnest, purposeful movement toward the remembrance of Allah, leaving aside worldly transactions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal physical hastening; metaphorical striving in obedience and abandoning distractions.\n- Root: عيد (ʿīd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A recurring festival or celebration; from the root meaning to return (ʿāda), signifying a day that returns annually or weekly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A day of gathering and joy appointed by Allah; for the Muslims, Friday is designated as a weekly festival (ʿīd) of communal worship and divine bounty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal recurring day of celebration; metaphorical reference to the eternal \"Day of Increase\" (Yawm al-Mazīd) in the Hereafter, a festival of divine vision.\n- Root: تجلى (tajallā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To manifest, reveal, disclose oneself; to appear clearly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The self-disclosure of the Divine to the blessed in Paradise; a theophany of grace, not of corporeal essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal unveiling or appearing; metaphorical for the beatific vision (ruʾyah) granted to the believers, a reality beyond spatial or material limitation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa al-Majrūr) — The separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb is permissible when the intervening clause is complete and meaningful, as in the structure of the ḥadīth, preserving clarity of syntax without ambiguity.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}], "10": [{"arabic": "قال أنس بن مالك في قوله تعالى فإذا قضيت الصلاة فانتشروا في الأرض وابتغوا من فضل الله أما إنه ليس بطلب دنيا لكن عيادة مريض وشهود جنازة وتعلم علم وزيارة أخ في الله عز وجل وقد سمى الله عز وجل العلم فضلا في مواضع قال تعالى وعلمك ما لم تكن تعلم وكان فضل الله عليك عظيما وقال تعالى ولقد آتينا داود منا فضلا يعني العلم فتعلم العلم في هذا اليوم وتعليمه من أفضل القربات والصلاة أفضل من مجالس القصاص إذ كانوا يرونه بدعة ويخرجون القصاص من الجامع بكر ابن عمر رضي الله عنهما إلى مجلسه في المسجد الجامع فإذا قاص في موضعه فقال قم عن مجلسي فقال لا", "english": "Know that the one who attends the Friday prayer should remain in the mosque until he prays the afternoon prayer (al-ʿaṣr); and if he remains until the sunset prayer (al-maghrib), that is even more excellent. It is said: \"Whoever prays the afternoon prayer in the congregational mosque (al-jāmiʿ) shall have the reward of the Pilgrimage (ḥajj); and whoever prays the sunset prayer therein shall have the reward of a Pilgrimage and an ʿUmra.\" However, if he does not feel secure from ostentation (al-taṣabbuʿ) and from harm befalling him through the gaze of people upon his seclusion (iʿtikāf), or he fears plunging into what does not concern him, then it is more excellent that he return to his house, remembering Allah (mighty and majestic), reflecting upon His graces (ālāʾihi), thanking Allah the Exalted for His granting of success (tawfīq), fearing his own shortcoming, and watching over his heart and his tongue until the setting of the sun — so that the noble hour (al-sāʿa al-sharīfa) does not pass him by. Nor should he speak in the congregational mosque or in other mosques about the affairs of this world. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: {«يَأْتِي عَلَى النَّاسِ زَمَانٌ يَكُونُ حَدِيثُهُمْ فِي مَسَاجِدِهِمْ أَمْرَ دُنْيَاهُمْ لَيْسَ لِلَّهِ تَعَالَى فِيهِمْ حَاجَةٌ فَلَا تُجَالِسُوهُمْ»} — \"A time will come upon people when their conversation in their mosques will be about their worldly affairs; Allah the Exalted has no need of them, so do not sit with them.\" (1)", "book_title": "Etiquette of Earning", "section_title_ar": "Section 150", "section_title_en": "Section 150", "section_index": 150, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Earning'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 10, 969], [2, 969, 1520], [3, 1520, 2079], [4, 2079, 2472], [5, 2472, 3824], [6, 3824, 4745], [7, 4745, 5138], [8, 5138, 5863], [9, 5863, 6520], [10, 6520, 7698], [11, 7698, 8755], [12, 8755, 9790]], "62:1": [[1, 10, 969], [2, 969, 1520], [3, 1520, 2079], [4, 2079, 2472], [5, 2472, 3824], [6, 3824, 4745], [7, 4745, 5138], [8, 5138, 5863], [9, 5863, 6520], [10, 6520, 7698], [11, 7698, 8755], [12, 8755, 9790]], "2": [[1,